1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,130
RelayCart connects safe incident response with customer recovery.

2
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,573
Start with a healthy service and a working checkout.

3
00:00:09,693 --> 00:00:14,061
The canary creates an order, establishing the business baseline.

4
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,591
Now deploy the broken release.

5
00:00:20,711 --> 00:00:25,271
Deployment completes, but checkout fails. That is the incident trigger.

6
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,362
n8n correlates deployment, health, and checkout signals.

7
00:00:33,482 --> 00:00:37,167
Duplicate signals do not create separate incidents.

8
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:41,085
One severity two incident captures the failure.

9
00:00:41,205 --> 00:00:45,666
The model response is invalid, so its advice is rejected.

10
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:50,086
The investigation gathers evidence and calls NVIDIA NIM.

11
00:00:50,177 --> 00:00:54,197
Invalid advice is rejected. Human approval stays enforced.

12
00:00:54,750 --> 00:01:02,510
The operator reviews the exact rollback, from version one point eight point two to one point
eight point one.

13
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:08,152
Approval is single use, expires, and is bound to this release change.

14
00:01:08,271 --> 00:01:12,679
The operator approves, then rollback and recovery checks execute.

15
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:18,266
The remediation workflow consumes approval and checks the source release again.

16
00:01:18,373 --> 00:01:21,200
A newer release would block a stale rollback.

17
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:27,057
Recovery checks the release, service health, checkout creation, and order read back.

18
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:34,106
All four checks pass before the incident becomes recovered.

19
00:01:34,226 --> 00:01:37,975
A green health check alone would not prove checkout works.

20
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:43,541
The customer impact workflow synchronizes live GoHighLevel cases.

21
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:46,698
Durable retries keep the handoff recoverable.

22
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:51,668
Eighteen checkout subscribers have Service Cases for this incident.

23
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:54,199
Historical cases are preserved.

24
00:01:54,750 --> 00:02:01,059
This published GoHighLevel workflow adds an intake note when a Service Case is created.

25
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:09,093
The technical recovery workflow updates the case and enrolls associated records in
confirmation.

26
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:13,542
Conversation AI asks whether checkout works again.

27
00:02:13,662 --> 00:02:18,179
Explicit branches handle working, still broken, and no response.

28
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:23,357
Here is the actual recovery question and LiveChat configuration.

29
00:02:23,467 --> 00:02:26,692
Silence cannot count as confirmed resolution.

30
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:32,686
Synthetic replies use the LiveChat test API to exercise real workflows.

31
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:37,288
n8n validates fresh replies and persists customer outcomes.

32
00:02:37,372 --> 00:02:41,194
Negative feedback checks for an existing task before creating one.

33
00:02:41,750 --> 00:02:44,299
Acme Bikes confirms checkout works.

34
00:02:44,408 --> 00:02:48,697
Its case is technically recovered and customer confirmed resolved.

35
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:51,634
Ocean Apparel still needs help.

36
00:02:51,754 --> 00:02:57,210
Technical status stays recovered while customer status becomes needs follow up.

37
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:02,874
One support task captures the unresolved customer issue.

38
00:03:02,994 --> 00:03:07,426
This count is scoped to the recorded incident; older tasks remain.

39
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:12,720
The final dashboard separates technical recovery from customer resolution.

40
00:03:12,813 --> 00:03:17,856
One customer is resolved, one needs follow up, and sixteen await confirmation.

41
00:03:18,417 --> 00:03:21,613
Verified recovery. Eighteen customer cases.

42
00:03:21,712 --> 00:03:24,849
A clear handoff for the customer who still needs help.
